An easy, ultra-creamy weeknight macaroni and cheese cooked right in the pressure cooker. Evaporated milk creates a rich emulsion that keeps the sauce silky and prevents breaking.

*Adapted from [Food Network](https://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/food-network-kitchen/5-ingredient-instant-pot-mac-and-cheese-3649854).*

## Ingredients

- 1 pound elbow macaroni
- 4 cups water
- 1 tablespoon kosher salt (plus more to taste)
- 1 can (12 oz) evaporated milk
- 3 tablespoons unsalted butter
- 3 cups shredded mild or medium cheddar cheese (or swap 1 cup for gouda or gruyère)
- ¼ cup water (optional, to thin sauce if desired)

## Instructions

1. **Pressure Cook the Macaroni:** Combine the elbow macaroni, 4 cups water, and 1 tablespoon kosher salt in a 6- or 8-quart Instant Pot. Lock the lid and set the steam release valve to Sealing. Pressure cook on HIGH for 4 minutes.
2. **Quick Release:** As soon as the 4-minute timer beeps, carefully perform a quick pressure release. Once the steam finishes venting and the safety float pin drops, unlock and remove the lid.
3. **Add the Liquids and Butter:** Switch the Instant Pot to the LOW Sauté setting. Pour in the can of evaporated milk, add the 3 tablespoons of butter, and stir in ¼ cup water. Stir gently until the butter melts into the warm pasta.
4. **Melt the Cheese:** Gradually add the shredded cheese one handful at a time, stirring continuously so each addition melts completely before adding more. Once smooth and emulsified, cancel the sauté function and serve immediately.

## Notes

- **Stovetop Modification:** You can easily make this recipe on the stovetop without a pressure cooker. Simply boil the 1 pound of macaroni in a large pot of boiling salted water until al dente, then drain thoroughly. Return the pasta to the warm pot over low heat on the stove, and follow the sauce instructions as written: stir in the evaporated milk, butter, and ¼ cup water, then gradually melt in the cheese while stirring constantly until rich and glossy.
- **Cheese Variations:** Mild or medium cheddar gives the classic smooth melt, but you can substitute 1 cup of cheddar with shredded gouda, fontina, or gruyère for extra flavor depth. Freshly grated cheese from a block melts significantly better than pre-shredded bagged cheese.
